<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#hattic-language">Glossary</a> · Term</p><h1 class="enc-title">Hattic language</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Hattic was the language of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hattians">Hattians</a> of central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a> in the 3rd and early 2nd millennium BCE. It is unrelated to the Indo-European Hittite language and to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/semitic-languages">Semitic languages</a> and has no known relatives. It survives mainly in ritual passages recorded in Hittite <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuneiform">cuneiform</a> texts from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hattusa">Hattusa</a>, as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hittite-empire">Hittites</a> preserved Hattic cults and gods.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hattic-language.jpg" alt="Hattic language" width="320" height="381" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Mx. Granger · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hittite_Cuneiform_Tablet-_Cultic_Festival_Script.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
